 "notes": "The list is on n51.jpg (p. 40), 33 rows, and n52.jpg (p. 41), 5 rows. n50.jpg (p. 39) has the section's opening text, and n53/n54 are past the end of the list. All rows sit under the section heading \"EAST COAST OF ENGLAND.\" There are no sub-headings and no station numbers.\n\nThe list doesn't print each light's character. The only note is the dagger after \"Longstone L.H.\", whose footnote on p. 40 reads: \"† For nature of light, position, and distance from the nearest land, see report for 1880.\" So that information is in the 1880 report, not here.\n\n\"L.H.\" and \"L.V.\" are printed in small capitals (l.h., l.v.); I have written them in capitals. Rows are joined to the keeper column by leader dots (\"...\"), and I have given the dots roughly as printed. Some rows have none before the keeper: Leman & Ower, Hasbro' L.V., Kentish Knock, Cockle and East Side. Rows with no keeper end in dots only.\n\nCounts: there are 38 stations, matching \"thirty-eight stations\" on p. 39. I count 27 stars (Longstone, Inner Farn, Tees, Coquet Island, Whitby High, Flamborough Head, Spurn L.H., Outer Dowsing, Inner Dowsing, Dudgeon, Hunstanton, Cromer, Leman & Ower, Hasbro' L.V., Newarp, Winterton, Cockle, Orfordness, Corton, Shipwash, Languard Point, Kentish Knock, Goodwin, Gull, East Side, Varne, Hanois). The text says \"returns have been received from twenty-six\", so the stars and the text disagree by one. The star before Newarp is set lower than the others but is still a star.\n\nCopied as printed, not corrected:\n- \"Llyn Wells\" (normally Lynn Well light-vessel)\n- \"Languard Point\" (Landguard; p. 40 also says \"Languard Lighthouse\")\n- \"Hasbro'\" for Haisborough/Happisburgh, both L.H. and L.V.\n- \"Orfordness\" as one word\n- \"W, Elsdon\", with a comma where a full stop belongs\n- \"J.N.Utting\", with no spaces\n- \"Perfrement\"\n- \"Edward le Gallais\", with a lower-case \"le\"\n\nCasquets and Hanois are Channel Islands lights, but they are printed under the East Coast heading. Only Hanois has a place, \"(Guernsey)\".\n\nPages 39–40 add two things. Thomas H. Cutting of Inner Farn sent nine schedules, the most from any one station, and Owen Boyle of Languard sent four. A \"F. G. Cutting\" is one of the three Hanois keepers.\n\nMarks that are not text:\n- A small pencil-like tick after the dots on the Nore row\n- A speck above the \"a\" of \"Casquets\"\n- A dot between \"J.\" and \"Rees\" on the Goodwin row\n\nNone of them changes a reading.\n\nThe OCR's \"Bare. Live jin f\" (line 3032) is the Nore L.V. row, and \"Hast Side\" is East Side. The OCR's Hanois keepers line is garbled; the image clearly reads \"Geo. Freeman, C. Williams, & F. G. Cutting.\"",
